title: One form, three materials
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

[[Emeco]] has made the same chair three times.

* 1944: the [[Navy Chair]]. Recycled aluminum, built by hand in Hanover, Pennsylvania. Tested to last 150 years.
* 2006: the [[111 Navy Chair]]. The same shape, molded from 111 recycled Coke bottles.
* 2019: the [[Navy Wood Chair (Amish)]]. The same shape in local wood, made with Amish woodworkers in Lancaster, Pennsylvania.

The shape never changes. What changes is where the material comes from, and where it can go when the chair is done.

The aluminum one can be melted and made again. That is a [[Technical Nutrient]] doing its job. See [[Aluminum]].

The bottle one gives plastic a second life. Does it get a third? See [[Downcycling]].

The wood one comes from the forest and the workshops down the road. It has the smallest [[Materialshed]] of the three, and it is the only one that could rot. See [[Biological Nutrient]].

So which one is the sustainable chair?

//(Yours to answer.)//

The [[Oka Chair]] goes the other way. It lets the material decide the shape.

